---
/**
 * Section — the shell every homepage and landing section sits in.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS. "The spacing looks wrong" was reported against the homepage
 * repeatedly, and it was never one section being wrong. It was that no two
 * sections agreed on anything: the audit found five different H2 sizes (56,
 * 46.4, 43.2, 41.6 and 60.8px at 1440), six different max-widths, five gutters,
 * and section padding that ranged from 48px to 160px with no rule behind it.
 * Fixing that section by section is how it got that way. It is fixed once, here,
 * and every section inherits it.
 *
 * WHAT IT OWNS, AND THEREFORE WHAT A SECTION MUST NOT REDECLARE:
 *   - the measure and gutter (--measure / --gutter)
 *   - vertical padding (--sec-pad-y)
 *   - the head block: eyebrow, heading, standfirst, and the gaps between them
 *   - the gap from the head block down to the content (--sec-gap)
 *
 * A section that sets its own max-width, its own H2 size, or its own top
 * padding is reintroducing the defect. If a section genuinely needs to differ,
 * use `bleed` for full-width media and record the reason in the call site.
 *
 * CENTRED HEAD BLOCK — owner decision, 2026-08-02: "all the eyebrow must be
 * gradient" and every page "must be transformed for centrally aligned".
 *
 * This file previously carried the opposite instruction, on the grounds that the
 * legacy stylesheet forced sixteen element types to text-align:center inside
 * every section and then needed 138 per-element opt-outs across 24 pages to
 * escape. That history is real and it is the reason for the SCOPE of the rule
 * below, not a reason to ignore the decision.
 *
 * WHAT IS CENTRED: the head block only — eyebrow, heading, standfirst — plus the
 * eyebrow capsule itself. That is what every approved Figma render shows.
 *
 * WHAT IS NOT: the slotted body. A centring rule that reaches into `.section__body`
 * would hit cards, tables, matrix rows, code blocks and prose, and re-create the
 * exact opt-out cascade this file was written to end. Structured content keeps
 * its own internal alignment. If a specific body needs centring, it centres
 * itself at its own call site, where the reason is visible.
 */
interface Props {
  /** Small mono line above the heading. Use it for provenance, not decoration. */
  eyebrow?: string;
  /** The section heading. Omit for a section that is purely visual. */
  title?: string;
  /** One or two sentences under the heading. Kept to ~62ch for readability. */
  standfirst?: string;
  /**
   * Heading level. STRUCTURE ONLY — it never changes the rendered size, because
   * heading level is document structure and --t-h2 is the section-heading size.
   *
   * THE FIRST SECTION OF A PAGE MUST PASS as="h1". This was missing when the
   * union allowed only h2 and h3, and the result was four pages built on this
   * primitive (contact, faq, about, partners) that shipped with NO h1 at all,
   * while every hand-built page on the site had one. A page with no h1 has no
   * document title in the accessibility tree and loses its strongest on-page
   * ranking signal, and nothing in the markup looks wrong — which is exactly why
   * tests/heading-structure.spec.js now asserts exactly one h1 on every route.
   */
  as?: 'h1' | 'h2' | 'h3';
  /** Anchor id. Also used to wire aria-labelledby. */
  id?: string;
  /**
   * Content escapes the measure and runs the full viewport width. For media
   * stages and bands only; the head block always stays on the measure so the
   * text never detaches from the page's vertical rhythm.
   */
  bleed?: boolean;
  class?: string;
}

const {
  eyebrow,
  title,
  standfirst,
  as: Heading = 'h2',
  id,
  bleed = false,
  class: className = '',
} = Astro.props;

/** Stable id for aria-labelledby, derived from the title when none is given. */
const slug =
  id ??
  (title
    ? title
        .toLowerCase()
        .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')
        .replace(/^-|-$/g, '')
    : undefined);
const headingId = slug ? `${slug}-title` : undefined;
---

<section
  class:list={['section', className]}
  id={slug}
  aria-labelledby={title ? headingId : undefined}
>
  {
    (eyebrow || title || standfirst) && (
      <div class="section__head">
        {eyebrow && <p class="section__eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>}
        {title && (
          <Heading class="section__title" id={headingId}>
            {title}
          </Heading>
        )}
        {standfirst && <p class="section__standfirst">{standfirst}</p>}
      </div>
    )
  }

  <div class:list={['section__body', { 'section__body--bleed': bleed }]}>
    <slot />
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .section {
    position: relative;
    padding-block: var(--sec-pad-y);
  }

  /* The measure, in one place. Everything on the page lines up because every
     section's text starts on this same vertical. */
  .section__head,
  .section__body {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: var(--gutter);
  }

  .section__body--bleed {
    max-width: none;
    padding-inline: 0;
  }

  /* Head block to content. The single gap that gives the page its rhythm. */
  .section__head + .section__body { margin-top: var(--sec-gap); }

  /* Owner decision 2026-08-02: head block centred on every page. Scoped to the
     three head elements so it cannot leak into slotted content. */
  .section__head { text-align: center; }

  /*
   * THE EYEBROW IS A CAPSULE, and the capsule is doing structural work rather
   * than decoration. Once the head block is centred there is no left edge to
   * start the label against, so a bare line of mono floats. The outline gives it
   * a shape to sit in, and the gradient then has a defined width to resolve
   * across instead of running to the text box.
   *
   * This replaces the 56px rule that used to sit above the label. The rule was
   * the left-aligned answer to the same problem and reads as a stray dash once
   * the block is centred.
   *
   * THE COLOUR IS A SECOND SIGNAL, NEVER THE ONLY ONE. It is a mono,
   * letter-spaced, uppercase label inside a visible outline; remove all colour
   * and it still reads as an eyebrow. WCAG 1.4.1.
   *
   * background-clip: text needs a transparent fill, and an inherited
   * -webkit-text-fill-color is exactly what caused this site's invisible-text
   * P0. It is scoped to this one short label, @supports-gated so a browser
   * without it keeps a solid readable colour, and every route runs axe.
   */
  .section__eyebrow {
    /* Inline-block, not fit-content on a block: the capsule has to hug its text
       AND be centred by the parent's text-align. A block with width:fit-content
       would need margin-inline:auto and would then ignore the head block's
       alignment, so the two rules could drift apart. */
    display: inline-block;
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    padding: 0.5rem 0.85rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-teal);
  }

  @supports ((background-clip: text) or (-webkit-background-clip: text)) {
    .section__eyebrow {
      background: var(--grad-spectrum);
      -webkit-background-clip: text;
      background-clip: text;
      color: transparent;
      -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    }
  }

  .section__title {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h2);
    font-weight: var(--t-w-h2);
    line-height: 1.12;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    /* ~22ch keeps a section heading to two lines at every width. Longer than
       that and it stops reading as a heading and starts reading as a sentence.
       Centred, so the measure has to be centred too or the heading sits left
       inside a centred block. */
    max-width: 22ch;
    margin-inline: auto;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .section__standfirst {
    margin: var(--stack-2) auto 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-lede);
    line-height: 1.65;
    max-width: 62ch;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }
</style>
